      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Can I have a word with you for a minute?' Vika turns a statement into a question purely through rising intonation on the word meaning 'may', followed by 'a minute'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Я слушаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am listening.' Kristina delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'I'm listening'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Ты много сделала.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You have done a lot.' The phrasing opens with 'a lot' before bringing in 'have done' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Но это прошлое.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'But this is the past.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be', connecting 'but' directly with 'last'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Нам нужны новые люди.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We need new people.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'needed' directly with 'new', combining 'needed' with 'new' and 'people'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Новая кровь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'New blood.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'new' directly with 'blood', combining 'new' with 'blood'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вы меня увольняете?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you firing me?' Vika turns a statement into a question purely through rising intonation on the word meaning 'are firing' to make the point directly in conversation.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Да.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Yes.' A crisp, everyday conversational reply meaning 'yes' that you will hear constantly in Russian dialogue.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Я надеюсь, ты найдешь работу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I hope you will find a job.' Kristina chooses the imperfective verb meaning 'hope so' for the ongoing action, followed by 'find' and 'job'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Не волнуйтесь за меня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Do not worry about me.' The reflexive ending on the verb for 'worry' turns the action back onto the subject. And Kristina jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Хорошо, не буду.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Okay, I won't.' The phrasing opens with 'good' before bringing in 'will be' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "До свидания.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
